@flexiarg argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts
@title Offer the Evidence the Venue Counts
@keywords venue, evidence, audience, measures, data
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the three LAK 2011 reviews of flop 1 (appendices/01, 'Reviews'); Joe's five-question analysis of flop 1 in the outtakes (appendices/07). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term argument-coherence/one-worked-instance argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  An argument addressed to a venue offers the kind of evidence that venue counts as support. A theory sent to an analytics venue needs measures, tools or data; a design sent to a funder needs a plan the funder can monitor.

  + context:
    Writers choose a venue for its topic and then argue in their own register. Reviewers judge by the evidence their field uses.

  + IF:
    An argument is aimed at a venue whose members weigh a particular kind of evidence.

  + HOWEVER:
    An early idea may have no such evidence yet. Then the honest move is a venue that takes early ideas, not a pitch that borrows the venue's vocabulary.

  + THEN:
    Name the venue's kind of evidence before writing, and either supply it or choose a venue that accepts what you have.

  + BECAUSE:
    Reviewers cannot weigh a claim in a currency they do not use. Flop 1 went to Learning Analytics with no data: 'How to quantify and measure'; 'What are tools for LA measurement?' (reviews). Joe's analysis: 'it is rather silly to target an analytics conference without concrete data', and later the ideas went to the Open Knowledge conference instead.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
    next[Related: writing-coherence/meet-the-reader-where-they-are covers the register; this pattern covers what counts as support.]
